\title{Non-embeddability of the Petersen Graph into Tropical Abelian Cayley Graphs}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-02}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We prove that the Petersen graph admits no isometric embedding into any Cayley graph of an abelian group whose connection set is the \emph{odd part} of an integer-valued tropical valuation. The argument factors into two logically independent components. The first is a purely metric obstruction, valid for any number of colors and any pair of graphs: an isometric map pulls back a proper $n$-coloring of the host to a proper $n$-coloring of the source. The second is a tropical bipartiteness certificate: for a Cayley graph whose generators are exactly the elements of odd valuation, the parity of the valuation is an additive character that properly two-colors the graph. Combining these with the classical fact that the Petersen graph is not bipartite---witnessed by an explicit five-cycle in its Kneser model $K(5,2)$---yields non-embeddability across an entire valuation-defined family of hosts simultaneously. We give a concrete instance on the integer lattice $\mathbb{Z}^k$ with the coordinate-sum valuation, and we relate the construction to the min-plus (tropical) semiring. We close with three conjectures delineating the boundary of the result.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The Petersen graph is the canonical small counterexample of graph theory: $3$-regular, vertex-transitive, of girth $5$ and diameter $2$, and famously \emph{not} a partial cube---it does not embed isometrically into any hypercube. This paper generalizes the non-partial-cube phenomenon in an algebraic and tropical direction. Rather than restricting attention to hypercubes (Cayley graphs of $(\mathbb{Z}/2)^k$ with the standard generators), we consider a broad family of \emph{tropical abelian Cayley graphs}, in which the generating set is produced by an integer-valued valuation on an abelian group, and we show that the Petersen graph is excluded from all of them at once.

The result rests on a clean dichotomy between metric and algebraic content. On the metric side, isometric embeddings are strong enough to transport proper colorings backward along the map; this is a statement about distances alone. On the algebraic side, the hosts we consider are bipartite for a structural reason: their edges are exactly the elements whose valuation is odd, and reducing the valuation modulo $2$ furnishes a proper two-coloring. The Petersen graph, being non-bipartite, cannot survive the transport, and non-embeddability follows.

\subsection*{Contributions}
\begin{itemize}
  \item A general metric obstruction (Theorem~\ref{thm:pullback}) and its contrapositive embedding-exclusion form (Theorem~\ref{thm:exclusion}), valid for all $n$ and all graphs.
  \item A self-contained proof that the Petersen graph is not two-colorable via an explicit odd cycle in the Kneser model $K(5,2)$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:petersen}).
  \item The definition of the \emph{odd-valuation Cayley graph} and a proof that it is bipartite, certified by the parity of the valuation (Theorem~\ref{thm:cert}).
  \item The main non-embeddability theorem (Theorem~\ref{thm:main}) and a concrete lattice instance (Corollary~\ref{cor:lattice}).
  \item A bridge to the min-plus semiring, situating the valuation's values in the tropical value group (Section~\ref{sec:bridge}).
  \item Three conjectures mapping the boundary of the obstruction (Section~\ref{sec:future}).
\end{itemize}

\section{Preliminaries and notation}

All graphs are simple and undirected. For a graph $G$ we write $V(G)$ for its vertex set, $u \sim_G v$ for adjacency, and $\dist_G(u,v)$ for shortest-path distance, with $\dist_G(u,v) = 1$ iff $u \sim_G v$.

A \emph{proper $n$-coloring} of $G$ is a function $c : V(G) \to \{1, \ldots, n\}$ with $c(u) \neq c(v)$ whenever $u \sim_G v$; $G$ is \emph{$n$-colorable} if such a coloring exists. A graph is \emph{bipartite} iff it is $2$-colorable.

\begin{fact}[Bipartiteness via closed walks]\label{fact:closedwalk}
A graph is $2$-colorable if and only if every closed walk has even length. Equivalently, it fails to be $2$-colorable iff it contains a closed walk of odd length.
\end{fact}

A map $f : V(G) \to V(H)$ is an \emph{isometric embedding} if
\[
  \dist_H\bigl(f(u), f(v)\bigr) = \dist_G(u,v) \qquad \text{for all } u, v \in V(G).
\]

\section{The tropical value algebra}

The \emph{min-plus} (tropical) semiring is $\Zbar = \mathbb{Z} \cup \{+\infty\}$ with tropical addition $x \oplus y = \min(x,y)$ and tropical multiplication $x \odot y = x + y$, additive identity $+\infty$, multiplicative identity $0$. Its central feature is \emph{idempotency}: $x \oplus x = x$. More abstractly, a linearly ordered idempotent commutative additive monoid $R$---one with $a + a = a$ and $a \le b \iff a + b = b$---has addition equal to a lattice operation,
\[
  a + b = \max(a,b),
\]
which specializes, under the appropriate orientation of the order, to the min or max of the tropical semirings. This is the algebra in which our valuations take values, and the natural setting for shortest-path distance: a walk's length is a tropical product (ordinary sum) of edge weights, and the distance is the tropical sum (extremal choice) over walks. Throughout, the value group is $\mathbb{Z}$, embedded in $\Zbar$.

\section{The metric obstruction}

\begin{theorem}[Coloring pullback under isometry]\label{thm:pullback}
Let $G$ and $H$ be graphs and $f : V(G) \to V(H)$ an isometric embedding, i.e.\ $\dist_H(f(u), f(v)) = \dist_G(u,v)$ for all $u, v$. If $H$ is $n$-colorable, then $G$ is $n$-colorable.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $c : V(H) \to \{1,\dots,n\}$ be a proper coloring of $H$. Define $c' := c \circ f$. Suppose $u \sim_G v$. Then $\dist_G(u,v) = 1$, and by the isometry hypothesis $\dist_H(f(u),f(v)) = 1$, so $f(u) \sim_H f(v)$. Since $c$ is proper, $c(f(u)) \neq c(f(v))$, i.e.\ $c'(u) \neq c'(v)$. Hence $c'$ is a proper $n$-coloring of $G$.
\end{proof}

The isometry hypothesis is used exactly once, to convert a $G$-edge into an $H$-edge; it is load-bearing, since a general map could crush a triangle onto an edge. Taking contrapositives:

\begin{theorem}[Embedding exclusion]\label{thm:exclusion}
If $G$ is not $n$-colorable and $H$ is $n$-colorable, then there is no isometric embedding $f : V(G) \to V(H)$.
\end{theorem}

We apply Theorem~\ref{thm:exclusion} with $n = 2$.

\section{The Petersen graph and its non-bipartiteness}

\begin{definition}[Petersen graph]
The vertex set is $V = \{\, s \subseteq \{0,1,2,3,4\} : |s| = 2 \,\}$, the ten two-element subsets of a five-element set. Two vertices $s, t$ are adjacent iff $s \cap t = \varnothing$.
\end{definition}

This is the Kneser graph $K(5,2)$: $3$-regular on $10$ vertices, girth $5$, diameter $2$.

\begin{theorem}[Petersen is not two-colorable]\label{thm:petersen}
The Petersen graph is not $2$-colorable.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Fact~\ref{fact:closedwalk} it suffices to exhibit an odd closed walk. Consider
\[
  \{0,1\} \to \{2,3\} \to \{4,0\} \to \{1,2\} \to \{3,4\} \to \{0,1\}.
\]
Each consecutive pair is disjoint, so each step is an edge, and the walk closes after five steps. Since $5$ is odd, the graph contains an odd closed walk and hence is not $2$-colorable.
\end{proof}

\section{Tropical abelian Cayley graphs}

Let $A$ be an additively written abelian group.

\begin{definition}[Cayley graph]
Given a symmetric connection set $S \subseteq A$ with $-s \in S$ for all $s \in S$ and $0 \notin S$, the \emph{Cayley graph} $\Cay(A, S)$ has vertex set $A$ and adjacency $g \sim h \iff h - g \in S$. Symmetry of $S$ makes the relation symmetric, and $0 \notin S$ makes it loopless.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Tropical valuation and odd-valuation Cayley graph]
A \emph{tropical valuation} on $A$ is a homomorphism $v : A \to \mathbb{Z}$, so $v(a+b) = v(a) + v(b)$, $v(0) = 0$, $v(-a) = -v(a)$. Its \emph{odd part} is
\[
  S_v := \{\, a \in A : v(a) \text{ is odd} \,\}.
\]
The \emph{odd-valuation Cayley graph} is $\Cay(A, S_v)$.
\end{definition}

The set $S_v$ is a legitimate connection set: symmetric since $v(-a) = -v(a)$ has the same parity as $v(a)$, and $0 \notin S_v$ since $v(0) = 0$ is even.

\begin{theorem}[Tropical bipartiteness certificate]\label{thm:cert}
The odd-valuation Cayley graph $\Cay(A, S_v)$ is $2$-colorable. The parity map $\chi : A \to \mathbb{Z}/2$, $\chi(g) = v(g) \bmod 2$, is a proper two-coloring and an additive character: $\chi(g+h) = \chi(g) + \chi(h)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
That $\chi$ is a homomorphism to $\mathbb{Z}/2$ follows from $v(g+h) = v(g)+v(h)$ modulo $2$. For properness, if $g \sim h$ then $h - g \in S_v$, so $v(h-g) = v(h) - v(g)$ is odd, whence $v(h)$ and $v(g)$ have opposite parities and $\chi(h) \neq \chi(g)$.
\end{proof}

The parity of the valuation both \emph{defines} the edge set and \emph{certifies} the two-coloring; this is where the tropical valuation controls the geometry.

\section{Main theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Non-embeddability]\label{thm:main}
The Petersen graph admits no isometric embedding into any odd-valuation Cayley graph $\Cay(A, S_v)$, for any abelian group $A$ and any tropical valuation $v : A \to \mathbb{Z}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:cert}, $\Cay(A, S_v)$ is $2$-colorable. By Theorem~\ref{thm:petersen}, the Petersen graph is not $2$-colorable. Theorem~\ref{thm:exclusion} with $n = 2$ rules out any isometric embedding.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Integer-lattice instance]\label{cor:lattice}
Take $A = \mathbb{Z}^k$ and $v(x_1,\dots,x_k) = \sum_i x_i$. Then $S_v$ consists of all integer vectors with odd coordinate sum, and $\Cay(\mathbb{Z}^k, S_v)$ is the bipartite integer lattice two-colored by the parity of the coordinate sum. The Petersen graph does not embed isometrically into it, in any dimension $k$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The coordinate sum is a homomorphism $\mathbb{Z}^k \to \mathbb{Z}$, hence a tropical valuation; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.
\end{proof}

\section{The min-plus bridge}\label{sec:bridge}

\begin{definition}[Tropical value of a valuation]
For $v : A \to \mathbb{Z}$, its \emph{tropical value} $\widetilde{v}(a) \in \Zbar$ is $v(a)$ regarded in the min-plus value structure.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Tropical combination rules]
In the idempotent min-plus algebra, for all $a, b$,
\[
  \widetilde{v}(a) \oplus \widetilde{v}(a) = \widetilde{v}(a), \qquad
  \widetilde{v}(a) \oplus \widetilde{v}(b) = \operatorname{ext}\bigl(\widetilde{v}(a), \widetilde{v}(b)\bigr),
\]
where $\operatorname{ext}$ is the extremal operation of the semiring.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Idempotency is $x \oplus x = x$ for $x = \widetilde{v}(a)$. The lattice identity is the fact that in a linearly ordered idempotent commutative monoid, $a + b = \max(a,b)$ (equivalently $\min$ under the reversed orientation).
\end{proof}

These identities make shortest-path distance in the host a genuinely tropical quantity, computed by iterated $\oplus/\odot$ over the value structure in which $v$ lands; the bipartiteness certificate is the reduction of this data modulo $2$.

\section{Discussion and future directions}\label{sec:future}

The obstruction separates into a valuation-free metric core (Theorem~\ref{thm:pullback}, any number of colors) and a one-parameter certificate (the parity of the valuation, Theorem~\ref{thm:cert}). The tropical content is confined to the certificate. What the argument does not cover is the case where the certificate degenerates---every generator of even valuation---motivating the following.

\begin{conjecture}[Even-valuation hosts]
There exists an abelian group $A$, a valuation $v$ all of whose generators have even value, and an isometric embedding of the Petersen graph into the associated Cayley graph. With all generators even the host may contain its own odd closed walks, removing the coloring obstruction.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Valuation-rank threshold]
For every abelian group with a valuation whose value group has rank one, no odd-girth graph embeds isometrically into the odd-valuation Cayley graph; but there is a rank-two valued group into which the Petersen graph does embed isometrically. Rank one forces a global linear order on generator lengths, propagating a parity obstruction along every closed walk; incomparable lengths in higher rank can cancel it.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Tropical distance spectrum]
The multiset of pairwise tropical path lengths realizable inside any odd-valuation Cayley graph omits at least one distance value the Petersen metric requires; the omission itself, not merely bipartiteness, is the true obstruction, and it persists for a positive-density family of non-bipartite hosts.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}

The metric rigidity underlying ``the Petersen graph is not a partial cube'' survives a change of the distance-generating algebra: replacing the classical $\mathbb{Z}/2$-character bipartiteness certificate by a tropical valuation rules out an entire valuation-defined family of Cayley hosts at once. The proof isolates a general metric obstruction from a tropical parity certificate, making transparent both why the exclusion holds and where its boundary lies.

